# I. SARABANDE  b1-34  (C minor, 3/4)
# The cellos sing the sarabande alone over a bare halo (2nd violins and
# violas sul tasto, basses pizzicato): period 1 ends on the dominant
# (b8).  The first violins take period 2, which climbs through the
# Neapolitan (b10) toward E-flat major and is turned back at the last
# moment: V7 of E-flat -> C minor (b15-16, the DECEPTIVE CADENCE).
# Out of that failed cadence an oboe begins a new, simpler tune in
# A-flat (b17); it, too, cadences deceptively (b20-21) and keeps
# climbing, the violins join it, the winds and horns gather, and the
# line arrives at the first climax: D-flat major (b25), the German sixth
# (b27, top e-flat 6), the cadential 6/4 and the dominant (b28-29).
# The climax is S's own bars 5-8, a fourth higher - the opening phrase
# finally sung at full voice.  Then a long exhale over a G pedal: the
# violin sighs down an octave and a half, the clarinet echoes it a bar
# behind, and the music stops on G - the same half cadence as bar 8.
# No trombones, no trumpets yet.

